  "body": "We talked about what to do with the promise that's created for the 'close' entry in the buffer, which ended up in bcf0040ff580fd265ed56acdb810270048a5d5d5, but upon testing my totally simple-minded sample stream, I discovered that trying to resolve on the promise returned by `close()` (which is done in several of the examples) fails because that promise is never resolved.\n\nI see two ways to resolve (heh) this:\n1. What I did, which was the simplest possible change, is to add a step after `[[doNextWrite]]` 1.ii where you resolve the close promise with a value of `undefined`.\n2. Don't create a new promise for the `close` entry, and instead return `[[closedPromise]]` from `close()`. This saves an object at the cost of making the behavior slightly less consistent and making the logic for `[[doNextWrite]]` slightly more complex.\n",
